-- title:
--   Maximal order has element with nrd exactly divisible by r
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb Q$ and let $q,q'$ be prime numbers, and consider the quaternion algebra $B=\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$. Assume `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`, that is: $0<a$ or $0<b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb Q$ the base change $B\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ (with $\mathbb Q_v$ the $v$-adic completion) is a division algebra — every nonzero element is a unit — exactly when $q\in v$ or $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $B$ which is a maximal order, meaning that $\Lambda$ contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $B$ as a $\mathbb Q$-vector space and is finitely generated over $\mathbb Z$, and that every submodule with these four properties containing $\Lambda$ equals $\Lambda$. Let $r\in\mathbb N$ with $r=q$ or $r=q'$. Then there exists $x\in\Lambda$ such that $\mathrm{nrd}\,x=r\,n$ for some $n\in\mathbb Z$, while there is no $n\in\mathbb Z$ with $\mathrm{nrd}\,x=r^{2}n$; here $\mathrm{nrd}\,x=x_{\mathrm{re}}^{2}-a\,x_{I}^{2}-b\,x_{J}^{2}+ab\,x_{K}^{2}$. In particular $\mathrm{nrd}\,x\neq 0$, so $x\neq0$, and the $r$-adic valuation of $\mathrm{nrd}\,x$ is exactly $1$. The sharper statement that $\mathrm{nrd}\,x$ can be taken equal to $r$ itself is not asserted.
--
--   At a prime $r$ where an indefinite rational quaternion algebra ramifies, the unique two-sided maximal ideal of the completed maximal order is generated by an element of reduced norm of valuation one; this is the integral form of that fact used in the project. It feeds the construction of submodules of index $r^2$ inside a maximal order and the study of the action on points of the fake elliptic curves occurring in the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of Shimura curves.
